    category: "practical",
    question: "PRACTICAL QUESTIONS\n\n### QUESTION 1: QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS\n\nYou are provided with the following:\nSolution Y: A 0.10 moldm⁻³ of HNO₃\nSolution Z: A 1.86 g of Na₂CO₃.nH₂O in 250 cm³ of solution\nMethylorange indicator.\n\n**Procedure:**\n(i). Pipette 25 cm³ of solution Z into a clean conical flask. Add 2 or 3 drops of indicator.\n(ii). Titrate against solution Y in the burette. Record your titre value.\n(iii). Repeat the titration two more times and find the average titre value.\n\n**From your result, calculate:**\n(i). number of moles of solutions Y and Z used.\n(ii). concentration in moldm⁻³ of anhydrous Na₂CO₃ in substance Z.\n(iii). concentration in gdm⁻³ of anhydrous Na₂CO₃ in substance Z.\n(iv). the numerical value of n in the formula. [10 Marks]\n\n**Burette Readings:**\n\n| | 1st Titration (cm³) | 2nd Titration (cm³) | 3rd Titration (cm³) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Final Reading | 30.10 | 32.00 | 30.00 |\n| Initial Reading | 0.00 | 2.00 | 0.00 |\n| Titre value | 30.10 | 30.00 | 30.00 |\n\nAverage titre value = (30.00 + 30.00)/2 = 30.00 cm³ [1 Mark]\n\n**N.B.:** If the difference between the obtained average titre and the actual value is:\n±0.2: 1 Mark is awarded\n±0.3: ½ Mark is awarded\n±0.4: 0 Mark is awarded\n\n**(ii). Balanced equation of reaction:**\n2HNO₃ + Na₂CO₃ → 2NaNO₃ + (n+1)H₂O + CO₂ [1 Mark]\n\n**(bi).** 100 cm³ of Y contains 0.10 mole of HNO₃\n30.0 cm³ of Y will contain 30 × 0.10/1000 = 0.003 mole of HNO₃ [½ Mark]\n\nFrom the balanced equation: 2 moles of HNO₃ required 1 mole of Na₂CO₃\n0.003 mole of HNO₃ will require 0.003 × 1/2 = 0.0015 mole of Na₂CO₃ [1 Mark]\n\n**(ii).** 25 cm³ of Z contains 0.0015 mole of Na₂CO₃\n1000 cm³ of Z will contain 1000 × 0.0015/25 = 15/25 = 0.06 moldm⁻³ of Na₂CO₃ [1 Mark]\n\n**(iii).** Molar mass of Na₂CO₃ = 2 × 23 + 12 + 3 × 16 = 106 gmol⁻¹\nConc. of Na₂CO₃ (gdm⁻³) = Conc. (moldm⁻³) × Molar Mass [½]\n= 0.06 × 106 = 6.34 gdm⁻³ [1 Mark]\n\n**(iv).** 250 cm³ of Z contains 1.86 g of Na₂CO₃.nH₂O\n1000 cm³ of Z will contain 1000 × 1.86/250 = 7.44 g of Na₂CO₃.nH₂O\nTherefore, mass conc. of Na₂CO₃.nH₂O is 7.44 gdm⁻³\n\nMass Conc. of Na₂CO₃.nH₂O / Mass Conc. of Na₂CO₃ = Molar Mass of Na₂CO₃.nH₂O / Molar Mass of Na₂CO₃\n\n7.44/6.34 = Molar Mass of Na₂CO₃.nH₂O / 106\nMolar Mass of Na₂CO₃.nH₂O = 7.44 × 106/6.34 = 788.64/6.34 = 116.6 [½ Mark]\n\n114.12n = 788.64 − 672.04 = 116.6\nn = 116.6/114.12 = 1.022 ≈ 1 [½ Mark]\n\n[Total = 10 Marks]\n\n---\n\n### QUESTION 2: QUALITATIVE ANALYSIS (2020)\n\nYou are provided with three liquid organic samples A, B and C and you are required to perform the following tests to identify the functional groups present in samples A, B and C.\n\n**Test 1:** Add 1 cm³ of samples A, B and C to separate test tubes and add 1 cm³ of 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine.\n\n**Test 2:** To fresh 1 cm³ of samples A, B and C in separate test tubes, add 1 cm³ of Tollen's reagent.\n\n**Test 3:** To fresh 1 cm³ of samples A, B and C in separate test tubes, add 1 cm³ of potassium dichromate solution followed by 1 cm³ of NaHCO₃.\n\n**Test 4:** To fresh 1 cm³ of samples A, B and C in separate test tubes, add 1 cm³ of NaOH.\n\nRecord your observations and inferences in each step.\nState the functional groups present in A, B and C.\nWrite the structural formulae of samples A, B and C if they contain three carbon atoms each. [10 Marks]\n\n**Answers — Qualitative Analysis (2020):**\n\nA = CH₃CH₂COOH (Propanoic acid)\nB = CH₃COCH₃ (Propanone)\nC = CH₃CH₂CHO (Propanal)\n\n| Question | Test | Observation | Inference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I | A + 2,4-DNPH | No visible reaction | Alkanal/alkanone absent |\n| I | B + 2,4-DNPH | Colour change from orange to yellow | Alkanal/alkanone present |\n| I | C + 2,4-DNPH | Colour change from orange to yellow | Alkanal/alkanone present |\n| II | A + Tollen's reagent | No visible reaction | Alkanal absent |\n| II | B + Tollen's reagent | No visible reaction | Alkanal absent |\n| II | C + Tollen's reagent | Silver mirror deposit | Alkanal present |\n| III | A + K₂Cr₂O₇/H⁺ + NaHCO₃ | No colour change; gas evolution | Alkanoic acid (-COOH) present |\n| III | B + K₂Cr₂O₇/H⁺ + NaHCO₃ | No colour change; No gas evolution | Alkanoic absent |\n| III | C + K₂Cr₂O₇/H⁺ + NaHCO₃ | Colour change from orange to green | Alkanal oxidized to alkanoic acid — ketone |\n| IV | A + I₂ + NaOH | No observable colour change | Methyl ketone absent |\n| IV | B + I₂ + NaOH | Formation of yellow precipitate | Methyl ketone present |\n| IV | C + I₂ + NaOH | Formation of yellow precipitate | Methyl present |\n\n**Functional groups:**\nA — Carboxyl group of alkanoic acid (—C(=O)—OH)\nB — Carbonyl group of alkanone (C=O with R group)\nC — Carbonyl group of alkanal (—C(=O)—H)\n\n[10 Marks]\n\n---\n\n### PRACTICAL QUESTION 1 (2020) — Carboxylic acids question:\n\n**(c).** Arrange the following carboxylic acids in order of increasing acidity. Justify your answer.\n3,3-dichloropentanoic acid; 2-chloropentanoic acid; 2,2-dichloropentanoic acid; 4-chloropentanoic acid. [2 Marks]\n\n**Answer:**\nPentanoic acid < 4-chloropentanoic acid < 3-chloropentanoic acid < 2-chloropentanoic acid < 2,2-dichloropentanoic acid [2 Marks]",
