{
      id: "che-2024-p0001",
      subject: "chemistry",
      year: 2024,
      category: "practical",
      question: "**Question 1: Quantitative Analysis**\n\nSolution A is a solution containing 15.00 cm³ concentrated acid HX (36% pure and 1.25 g/cm³ density).\nSolution B is a solution that contains 1.08 g of sodium trioxocarbonate (IV), Na2CO3 in 200 cm³ of solution.\n\nTitration with methyl orange indicator:\n\n| Burette readings | 1st Titration (cm³) | 2nd Titration (cm³) | 3rd Titration (cm³) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Final Reading | 14.00 | 27.80 | 41.60 |\n| Initial Reading | 0.00 | 14.00 | 27.80 |\n| Titre value | 14.00 | 13.80 | 13.80 |\n\nAverage titre = (13.80 + 13.80)/2 = **13.80 cm³**\n\nReaction: 2HX + Na2CO3 → 2NaCl + H2O + CO2\n\n(bi) 200 cm³ of B contains 1.08 g of Na2CO3\n1000 cm³ contains (1.08 × 1000)/200 = 5.40 gdm⁻³\nMolar mass of Na2CO3 = 2×23 + 12 + 3×16 = 106 gmol⁻¹\nConcentration of B = 5.40/106 = **0.0509 moldm⁻³**\n\n(ii) 1000 cm³ of B contains 0.0509 mol of Na2CO3\n25 cm³ of B contains (25 × 0.0509)/1000 = 1.2725 × 10⁻³ mol of Na2CO3\nFrom the balanced equation: 1 mol Na2CO3 reacts with 2 moles HX\n1.2725 × 10⁻³ × 2 = 2.545 × 10⁻³ mol of HX in average titre\n\n13.80 cm³ of A contains 0.002545 mol of HX\n1000 cm³ of A contains (0.002545 × 1000)/13.80 = **0.1844 moldm⁻³**\n\n(iii) 1 cm³ of A contains 1.25 g of impure HX\n36/100 × 1.25 = 0.45 g of pure HX\n1000 cm³ of A contains 450 g of pure HX\nC1 = 450 gdm⁻³; V1 = 15 cm³; V2 = 1000 cm³\nC2 = C1V1/V2 = (450 × 15)/1000 = 6750/1000 = **6.75 gdm⁻³**\n\n(iv) Molar mass of HX = 6.75/0.1844 = **36.6 gmol⁻¹**\n1 + X = 36.6; X = 35.6\nTherefore relative atomic mass of X is 35.6 → **X is Chlorine; HX is HCl**\n\n---\n\n**Question 2: Qualitative Analysis**\n\n**Sample P (first table):**\n\n| Test | Observation | Inference |\n|---|---|---|\n| P + heat | Colourless alkaline gas with choking smell; white fumes with HCl | Gas is NH3; NH4⁺ ion present |\n| P + H2O | Sample dissolves completely | — |\n| P solution + NaOH in drops and excess | Effervescence of colourless gas, turns red litmus blue | Gas is ammonia from NH4⁺ |\n| P solution + dil. HCl + BaCl2(aq) | White precipitate formed | Precipitate of BaSO4; SO4²⁻ confirmed |\n\nIons in sample P are NH4⁺ and SO4²⁻\n(NH4)2SO4(aq) + 2NaOH(aq) → Na2SO4(aq) + 2NH3(g) + H2O(l)\n\n**Sample Q (second table):**\n\n| Test | Observation | Inference |\n|---|---|---|\n| Q + water | Q dissolves completely | — |\n| Q solution + NaOH in drops and excess | Green precipitate, insoluble in excess NaOH | Precipitate of Fe(OH)2; Fe²⁺ present |\n| P solution + NH3 in drops and excess | Green precipitate, insoluble in excess; turns brown on standing | Fe²⁺ present; brown due to formation of ferric ion |\n| P solution + K4Fe(CN)6(aq) | Dark or Prussian blue | Precipitate of Fe[2Fe(CN)6](s); Fe²⁺ confirmed |\n\n**(c)** The formula of the compound formed is **Fe(NH4)2(SO4)2.6H2O**",
      difficulty: "medium"
    }
